Kevin Esvelt, inventor of clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)-based gene drives, described how his second-year graduate student successfully synthesised an influenza virus from scratch using only online protocols, despite having no prior virology experience. When asked if his other students could design components to recreate the 1918 flu strain, Esvelt noted “[they all could](https://80000hours.org/podcast/episodes/kevin-esvelt-stealth-wildfire-pandemics/).”  
